Example 14.3 : In example 14.2 if we start with initial concentration of 0.50 how long will it take for this concentration to drop to 0.10 M.
Solution : We have to find the time taken by the reaction starting from concentration of H_2O_2 of 0.5M to concentration of 0.1 M.
We have the equation,
k = \frac{2.303}{t} \log \frac{[A]_0}{[A]}
Substituting the values of k, [A]_0 and [A] we have
0.0410\,\mathrm{min^{-1}} = \frac{2.303}{t}\log\frac{0.50}{0.10}
t = \log 5 \times \frac{2.303}{0.0410 \,\text{min}^{-1}} = \frac{0.699 \times 2.303}{0.041} \,\text{min}
t = 39 \text{ minutes}

Example 14.5: What must be the value of E_a if the rate constant for a reaction is doubled when the temperature increases from 300K to 310K?
Solution : Given \frac{k_2}{k_1} = 2, R = 8.31 JK-1
T_2 = 310 \text{ K}
T_1 = 300 \text{ K}
E_a = ?
We have equation:
\log \frac{k_2}{k_1} = \frac{E_a}{2.303 \,\text{R}} \left( \frac{T_2 - T_1}{T_1 \cdot T_2} \right)
substituting the values we have
\log 2 = \frac{E_a}{2.303 \times 8.31 \,\text{JK}^{-1}} \frac{(310 - 300) \,\text{K}}{(310 \,\text{K} \times 300 \,\text{K})}
solving, we have
E_a = 53.5 \text{ kJ}

